00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:07.060
Is Coronavirus the end of New York as we know it? That's exactly what Urbanist Joel Kotkin argued

00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:13.820
in a recent piece for Tablet magazine. This is happening at a time when the demographics of cities like New York,

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:20.100
LA, Chicago, San Francisco, are all going in the wrong direction, in other words young people are leaving,

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:26.360
population growth is very low, I think the pandemic is just one more factor.

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:31.940
With lockdowns  and extreme social distancing bringing urban life to a halt, and New York emerging

00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:36.300
as the epicenter of the national crisis, a debate among urban studies scholars

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:40.320
is breaking out over what this will mean for the future of American cities.

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:45.640
On one side our density skeptics like Kotkin, who believes the pandemic will only accelerate

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:52.000
an already present decline in urban living. While pro density urbanists, like University of Toronto Professor

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:56.940
Richard Florida, say that New York will do what New York has always done: bounce back.

00:00:56.940 --> 00:01:03.580
Biggest mistake people ever make is counting New York out. If there's one place in the world

00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:05.900
don't count out ever—

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:11.220
Thomas Campanella, a Cornell professor who co-edited a book called the  resilient city after 9/11,

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:15.740
agrees with Florida. No we're not looking at the end of New York by any means,

00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:21.280
nor are we looking at the end of cities. Very similar predictions were made in the wake of 9/11,

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:26.680
and there was dispersal certainly. Firms in lower Manhattan decamped to the suburbs,

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:32.920
they went across the river, elsewhere, but many of them came back and New York boomed as it's never

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:37.200
done before. In the book Campanella
and his co-editor document cities that

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:40.890
did and didn't bounce back from
catastrophe and find the disasters that

00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:44.759
damaged critical infrastructure such as
the volcanic eruption that buried

00:01:44.759 --> 00:01:48.540
Pompeii or make land literally
uninhabitable such as the nuclear

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:52.590
meltdown in Chernobyl are most likely to
do irreparable harm cities have

00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:57.210
recovered from pandemics fairly quickly
including New York after the 1918

